bevy-horizon: NAK_DEBUG=xbar, scoreboards that stay in flight across forward branches (SM50-SM6x)

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at d4a00ea0ab3f59afb967cc5d779e4263d237bd77, after 0001 to 0003. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies
it; this text before the first "diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

On Maxwell and Pascal, NAK's assign_barriers() (calc_instr_deps.rs) waits on every outstanding
scoreboard at every branch, including the SYNC that ends each arm of an if/else or switch. A
fragment shader that samples a texture or loads from a buffer inside such an arm waits out the
whole latency of that load before it leaves the arm, so loads in different arms never overlap.

With NAK_DEBUG=xbar, assign_barriers_xbar() instead leaves scoreboards in flight across a BRA or
SYNC that only goes forward, and waits where the result is used, as it already does inside a
block. Loops (back-edges), BRK, CONT and EXIT still wait on everything. It relies on two things:
a scoreboard counts outstanding operations, so several can share one (nv50_ir's GM107 emitter
shares SB5 the same way), and an operation issued under a divergent mask writes only the lanes
that issued it, so the other side of the branch can't see its result early.

It is opt-in because it has only run under Ryujinx, which ignores scoreboards, so nothing yet
shows that it is right on hardware. Every shader's result is checked by xbar_verify(), a hazard
check over the whole CFG that doesn't share code with either pass. If that check fails, or
rejects assign_barriers()'s own result, the shader keeps assign_barriers()'s result and NAK
prints "NAK xbar: kept legacy barriers: <reason>". NVK's shader cache key includes the NAK_DEBUG
flags, so shaders built with and without xbar are cached separately.

NAK_DEBUG=stats also gains waits= (instructions that wait on a scoreboard) and bra_waits=
(branches among them), which show the difference. NAK_DEBUG=final prints each shader's final IR
once, between "NAK_FINAL begin" and "NAK_FINAL end" lines, with the scoreboards every instruction
sets (rd:, wr:) and waits on (wt=); "print" shows the same after every pass, which takes minutes
for a large shader on the Switch's CPU. The unit tests at the end of
calc_instr_deps.rs build CFGs by hand and check both the passes and the verifier.

